---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const rows = [
  ["Document", "Pasaporte Argentino, Série A No. 07962; passport no. 2/1940"],
  ["Issued by", "Oficina Consular Argentina en Cape Town, Unión de Sud Africa"],
  ["Date", "24 DIC 1940"],
  ["Bearer", "Lerena, Pablo Armando"],
  ["Nationality", "Argentina — nativo (native-born, not naturalised)"],
  ["Born", "Rosario de Santa Fe, República Argentina — día 22, mes Febrero, año 1882"],
  ["Domiciled", "Rugby, Cape Town"],
  ["Travelling to", "Portuguese East Africa"],
  ["Profession", "Trainer"],
  ["Marital status", "Viudo — widower"],
  ["Description", "Skin blanco · eyes azules · hair gris · nose recta · height 1 m. 80"],
  ["Distinguishing marks", "Cicatriz en la frente — a scar on the forehead"],
  ["Military service", "Hizo su servicio militar en la Guardia Nacional, 6º de Caballería (1915)"],
  ["Signed", "A. B. Bayne, Encargado, Consulado General Argentino"],
  ["Also", "Photograph under an embossed consular seal; right thumbprint; 16.50-peso consular stamp"],
];
---
<Base title="The passport">
  <h1>The passport</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An Argentine passport issued at Cape Town on Christmas Eve 1940 gives a birthplace of
    <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe</strong> and a profession in one word — <strong>Trainer</strong>. It is the
    only document here that names an exact Argentine town. Whose it was is a harder question than it looked.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Pasaporte Argentino, Série A No. 07962</strong>,
      held in the family's own papers. Photographed and read 8 September 2026; both scans are preserved in
      this project at <span class="mono">sources/pablo-armando-lerena/</span>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What it says</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><th style="width:11rem">{k}</th><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What it settles</h2>

  <h3>Rosario — exactly as the family said</h3>
  <blockquote>Nacido en <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe, República Argentina</strong> · Día 22, Mes Febrero, Año <strong>1882</strong></blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The family remembered an Argentine great-grandfather and named
    <strong>Rosario</strong> for him. A passport in his own hands gives Rosario de Santa Fe and the date
    <strong>22 February 1882</strong>. The tradition was accurate, and this archive's
    <a href={u("/argentina")}>Argentina page</a> is no longer a page of method waiting for a subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    He is recorded as <strong><em>nativo</em></strong> — Argentine by birth, not naturalised. Whatever
    brought the family to the River Plate, it happened in an earlier generation than his.
  </p>

  <h3>The horses — no longer family lore</h3>
  <blockquote>Profesión: <strong>Trainer</strong></blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The word is written in English on a Spanish form, which is what
    a Cape Town clerk would do with a profession that had no neat Spanish equivalent on the page. The family
    said he brought horses; his own passport calls him a <strong>trainer</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the horses reach back to Argentina. Under military service the passport records that he served in
    the <strong>Guardia Nacional, 6º de Caballería</strong> — the <strong>6th Cavalry</strong> — in
    <strong>1915</strong>. He was a horseman in Argentina before he was a trainer at the Cape.
    <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h3>Rugby — the same street as the stables</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> He gives his address as <strong>Rugby, Cape Town</strong>. The
    South African National Archives hold a 1931 Cape Town municipal file:
    <em>plans of proposed <strong>stables</strong>, Koeberg Road, <strong>Rugby</strong> — RP Lerena</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two independent sources, found on different days from different archives, put a Lerena and a set of
    stables in the same small suburb. <a href={u("/naairs")}>What the archives returned &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not settle</h2>

  <h3>He was not the Consul General — at least not on this day</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip dis">Disputed</span> The family holds that he was <strong>Consul General</strong>
    between Argentina and South Africa. This document does not support that, and it is the kind of document
    that would.
  </p>
  <p>
    He appears here as the <strong>bearer</strong> of a passport, not its issuer. The signature in the
    consul's box is <strong>A. B. Bayne</strong>, <em>Encargado</em> — the officer in charge of the
    Consulado General Argentino at Cape Town in December 1940. Pablo Armando Lerena is the private citizen
    on the other side of the counter.
  </p>
  <p>
    That does not disprove the tradition outright. He may have held an honorary post at another date, and an
    Argentine national of standing resident in Cape Town is exactly the profile from which honorary consuls
    were drawn — a hypothesis this archive
    <a href={u("/argentina")}>recorded before the document was found</a>. But the claim now has a document
    pointing the other way, and it is demoted accordingly until something else says otherwise.
  </p>

  <h3>Who was he? &mdash; now the archive's sharpest question</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Open</span> When this page was first written, Pablo Armando Lerena was the only named
    Lerena in the archive and was assumed to head the line. Documents found the same day have displaced him
    from that position without explaining who he was.
  </p>
  <p>The head of the family is now securely <a href={u("/estate")}><strong>Roberto Paul Lerena</strong></a>,
    and the two men do not reconcile:</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Pablo Armando Lerena</th><th>Roberto Paul Lerena</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Born</th><td>22 February <strong>1882</strong>, Rosario de Santa Fe</td>
          <td>about <strong>1874</strong> (aged 76 in March 1950), "South America"</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Trade</th><td><strong>Trainer</strong></td><td><strong>Race Horse Owner</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>In 1940</th><td>A <strong>widower</strong>, living at <strong>Rugby</strong></td>
          <td>A <strong>widower</strong> since 1935; had built stables at <strong>Rugby</strong> in 1931</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Names</th><td>Pablo &middot; <strong>Armando</strong></td>
          <td>Roberto &middot; <strong>Paul</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Pablo</strong> is simply the Spanish for <strong>Paul</strong>, and both men are South American
    widowers in the horse business living in the same small Cape Town suburb. But
    <em>Roberto</em> is not <em>Armando</em>, and eight years is a wide gap even for an age reported at
    death.
  </p>
  <p>
    The likeliest reading is that they were <strong>brothers</strong> — two Argentine horsemen who came to
    the Cape together, which would explain why one man's passport is still in the other's family papers. It
    would also explain a name: Roberto Paul's eldest son was christened Roque Luis
    <strong>Armando</strong> Lerena. <span class="chip">Inferred, and thinly</span>
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way, the passport's value is undiminished. It is the only document in this archive that names an
    exact Argentine birthplace, and if the two men were brothers then
    <strong>Rosario is this family's town</strong> either way. <a href={u("/argentina")}>Argentina &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>The smaller things</h2>
  <p>
    He was a <strong>widower</strong> by December 1940, so his wife died before that date. He was travelling
    to <strong>Portuguese East Africa</strong> — Mozambique — in the second winter of the war, presumably on
    racing business. He stood 1.80 m, had blue eyes and grey hair, and carried a
    <strong>scar on his forehead</strong> that nobody now living put there.
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    The passport also carries his right thumbprint and his signature. It is the most personal document in
    this archive.
  </p>
</Base>
